1.7: sparse_attn stages the gathered KV row in shared memory -- 4.227 +/- 0.121 ms per forward, 16 of 16 legs byte-identical, and it is the first item on this ladder to move term A

WHAT WAS MEASURED. One server load per arm, control arm FIRST so drift favours the control, four
reps x three sweep contexts plus two control contexts. Control arm is
`DSV4_SPARSE_HPB=1 DSV4_SPARSE_SMEM=0`, the pre-1.7 launch bit for bit, on the same binary.

  ctx     ms/forward before -> after   paired    tok/s before -> after      tau
  1,656   133.46 -> 130.33             -3.13     13.93 -> 14.27 (+2.44 %)   1.861 both
  3,197   136.89 -> 132.78             -4.10     12.07 -> 12.44 (+3.07 %)   1.652 both
  6,248   141.46 -> 137.05             -4.40     11.85 -> 12.23 (+3.21 %)   1.681 both
  6,260   138.49 -> 134.20             -4.29     11.74 -> 12.13 (+3.32 %)   1.620 both
  12,410  146.90 -> 142.24             -4.66     11.71 -> 12.09 (+3.25 %)   1.736 both

Every one of the 16 legs is faster, and all 16 emitted a BYTE-IDENTICAL text_sha256 with tau and
mean verify width equal to four decimals -- at ctx up to 12,282, not at ctx 9. This satisfies the
ladder's PRIMARY bit-exactness invariant rather than its lossless-gate fallback.

WHAT IT MEANS. Split at the knee the cost model predicts (topk = WINDOW(128) +
min(INDEX_TOPK(512), ctx/ratio) saturates at ctx 2048): -3.127 +/- 0.014 ms below it, -4.384 +/-
0.065 above it, and above it delta = -3.996 +/- 0.080 flat -0.0552 +/- 0.0102 per 1000. So 94 % of
the win is the FLOOR -- which is exactly what this ladder entry predicted when it wrote "the lever
here is the 11 ms FLOOR, not the slope, and it belongs to Term A as much as to Term B".

  a  129.11 -> 125.11 ms   (1.571x -> 1.522x the 82.18 ms byte floor; stop wants <= 1.25)
  b  1.942  -> 1.887 /1000 (b x 6592: 12.80 -> 12.44 ms; stop wants <= 5.0; -74 % cumulative)

Six items have moved b by 74 %. This is the first to move a at all, and a is 125.11 of a 142.24 ms
forward at ctx 12,410 -- 88 % -- so it is where the remaining headroom is.

The 6 % that is not flat was PREDICTED FROM THE LAYER MAP BEFORE IT WAS FITTED, which is the only
thing separating it from the artefact rule 7 exists to catch: 20 of 43 layers are ratio-128, and
their topk = wmax + ctx/128 has no index_topk cap, so 20 x 7.81 rows per 1000 x 0.309 us saved per
row = 0.048 ms per 1000 predicted against 0.055 +/- 0.010 measured.

THE MECHANISM, and the part worth remembering: THE HYPOTHESIS IN THE LADDER ENTRY WAS WRONG. Both
the entry and B9's own comment blamed REUSE -- num_key_value_heads == 1, so topk_idxs is indexed by
(b,m) and not by head, all 64 heads gather the identical rows, 63/64ths of 168 MB per compressed
layer is redundant. `hpb`, which is precisely the fix for that, already existed, and it is a
MEASURED NULL: 1.00x at hpb=2 and hpb=4 at all six engine shapes. L1 was already catching it. The
binding constraint was instruction ISSUE: 32 scalar loads per warp per gathered row on a kernel
with 3-6 warps per SM and a 5-deep __shfl_down_sync chain with nothing to hide them behind. The fix
is to stage the row in shared memory once per block with float4 loads and double buffering, hold it
in registers across both consumers, and skip the expf when the running max did not move.

VECTORISING THE GLOBAL LOAD WITHOUT LOSING BIT-EXACTNESS IS THE WHOLE TRICK. float4 normally
destroys it, because it changes which lane owns which element and therefore the shape of the
partial-sum tree. Staging through smem decouples them: the load pattern becomes a pure
memory-PLACEMENT decision while the reduction still sees lane l holding {l, l+32, ..., l+480}, the
same 5-step shuffle tree, and the same online-softmax order over t. Every float operation, in every
order, is the one the shipped kernel performed.

AND THE OLD DEFAULT WAS A LIVE REGRESSION AT ITS OWN DESIGN POINT. B9 sized the hpb heuristic on a
1022-token prefill and shipped HPB=8 there; measured today at that exact shape it is 0.80x, and at
the K=6 verify width 0.52x. Nothing had re-run the sweep after the shape moved. The new default
(hpb=4, smem=2 below 1024 warps, smem=1 above) is 1.28x at that prefill and 1.21x at the short one,
so this item also gives prefill back the 20 % the heuristic had been quietly costing it.

THE GATES. tests/gate_sparse_hpb.cu memcmps the ENTIRE output buffer of every (hpb, smem) launch
against the pre-1.7 launch at six engine shapes -- 0 differing bytes -- and runs a one-ulp negative
control per shape that must fail. A cosine gate would pass exactly the defect this must catch,
because sparse_attn sums the gathered rows IN ORDER under a non-associative online softmax. That
control earned itself immediately: on its first run it came back "0 of 65536 floats differ" at
topk=320, because the row it perturbed was not in that shape's gathered set -- a perturbation the
kernel provably cannot see is not a control. Then gate_units, gate_indexer_decode,
gate_compressed_decode, gate_prefill_len, gate_compressed_graph and gate_indexer_graph all at
maxabs = 0.00e+00; LOSSLESS GATE -> PASS x3; and both build/decode arms generated the identical ids
11111 16 455 6102 294 16603 344 29168.

dprof, both arms, ctx 3072/6144/12,288: cattn:sparse 18.66/19.38/20.57 -> 15.27/14.48/15.38, with
the parent ATTENTION falling 5.75 against the mark's 5.19 at ctx 12,288 and i:score, i:topk, o:rope
and every moe:* mark unchanged -- no work relocated into an unmarked region. The before-arm also
reproduces 0.4's original 19.22/19.90/21.17 four iterations and four kernel changes later.

Wiki, same iteration: kernel-optimisations.md 2.9 (adopted win), negative-results.md 4g (hpb alone,
and the reuse hypothesis), measurement-and-traps.md 28 (a tuning default that expired),
context-scaling.md (the 6 % Term-B tail), prefill-optimisation.md 7, README.md state table.

Driver scripts/sparse_ab_run.sh, detached and named in detach_audit.sh's PATTERNS in this commit.

